{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Raise the personal allowance to £10,000 with £15bn in identified savings and banking reform; protect frontline NHS services and integrate health and social care; a one‑year jobs and green stimulus to create 100,000 jobs; political reform (fair votes, recall, Freedom Bill); targeted education investment via a £2.5bn Pupil Premium. TEXT_C: Declare Britain a non‑immigration country and halt all further immigration; immediate EU withdrawal; ban the burka and new mosques; restore corporal and capital punishment; end foreign aid; raise the personal allowance to £12,500; make the NHS serve British people and cut bureaucracy.",
  "reasoning": "In 2010 voters’ top concerns centered on the economy (taxes, deficit, jobs), the NHS/public services, and cleaning up politics after the expenses scandal. TEXT_D directly targets these with concrete tax, deficit, jobs, NHS, and political‑reform measures, whereas TEXT_C is dominated by immigration/EU/cultural policies that, while salient, were not the primary electorate priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
